1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,077
Your mortgage payment this
month is one thousand,

2
00:00:03,077 --> 00:00:05,178
four hundred and sixty-one pounds.

3
00:00:05,178 --> 00:00:08,611
Four hundred and twenty pounds

4
00:00:08,611 --> 00:00:11,367
of that buys your house.

5
00:00:11,367 --> 00:00:15,629
The other one thousand
and forty-two is rent —

6
00:00:15,629 --> 00:00:18,832
on money. Not on the property.

7
00:00:18,832 --> 00:00:22,226
On the money. Run that forward.

8
00:00:22,226 --> 00:00:25,382
Ten years in — a hundred
and twenty payments,

9
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:28,980
never missed — you have
handed over a hundred and

10
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:32,100
seventy-five thousand pounds.

11
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,116
And you still owe a hundred
and eighty-four thousand.

12
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:40,355
You have paid more than you have cleared.

13
00:00:40,355 --> 00:00:43,791
Over the full twenty-five years
the interest alone comes to

14
00:00:43,791 --> 00:00:47,057
a hundred and eighty-eight
thousand pounds.

15
00:00:47,057 --> 00:00:48,319
That is a second house.

16
00:00:48,319 --> 00:00:50,797
You just don't get to live in it.

17
00:00:51,616 --> 00:00:55,075
Most people assume that's
just what a mortgage

18
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:58,168
is. It isn't. It's one specific structure,

19
00:00:58,168 --> 00:01:01,650
and there are three others
that are used every day by

20
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:05,220
Islamic banks in Britain,
America and the Gulf.

21
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:09,094
In the next twelve minutes:
why the conventional mortgage

22
00:01:09,094 --> 00:01:13,179
is shaped the way it is, what
the three halal alternatives

23
00:01:13,179 --> 00:01:15,806
actually do differently — Murabaha,

24
00:01:15,806 --> 00:01:18,685
Ijara, and Diminishing Musharaka —

25
00:01:18,685 --> 00:01:22,548
and the four questions that
separate a genuine Islamic mortgage

26
00:01:22,548 --> 00:01:25,695
from a conventional loan
wearing Arabic vocabulary.

27
00:01:26,384 --> 00:01:29,321
Here's the part that gets skipped.

28
00:01:29,321 --> 00:01:32,782
When you take a conventional
mortgage, the bank does not buy

29
00:01:32,782 --> 00:01:35,112
you a house. The bank lends you cash.

30
00:01:35,112 --> 00:01:36,703
You buy the house.

31
00:01:36,703 --> 00:01:40,080
The bank takes a legal
charge over it as security.

32
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,638
That distinction is everything.

33
00:01:42,638 --> 00:01:45,267
The bank never owns the property.

34
00:01:45,267 --> 00:01:47,958
It never carries the risk of owning it.

35
00:01:47,958 --> 00:01:50,566
If the roof fails, that's yours.

36
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:53,356
If the market collapses, that's yours.

37
00:01:53,356 --> 00:01:56,226
If the building burns down uninsured,

38
00:01:56,226 --> 00:01:57,710
you still owe every penny.

39
00:01:57,710 --> 00:02:01,206
What the bank owns is a claim on you.

40
00:02:01,206 --> 00:02:04,628
And that claim grows
at a fixed percentage,

41
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:08,161
guaranteed, regardless of what
happens to the asset or to you.

42
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:11,653
Money lent, more money
back, no ownership and

43
00:02:11,653 --> 00:02:14,258
no risk taken in between.

44
00:02:14,258 --> 00:02:16,058
That is the definition.

45
00:02:16,058 --> 00:02:18,036
Not the marketing — the mechanism.

46
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:22,028
Now watch how it's sequenced.

47
00:02:22,028 --> 00:02:25,079
Interest is charged on what you still owe.

48
00:02:25,079 --> 00:02:28,225
At the start you owe almost everything,

49
00:02:28,225 --> 00:02:31,680
so almost everything you pay is interest.

50
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:35,966
In year one, seventeen and a
half thousand pounds leaves your

51
00:02:35,966 --> 00:02:39,339
account. Twelve thousand three
hundred of it is interest.

52
00:02:39,339 --> 00:02:41,467
Seventy-one percent.

53
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:44,901
You are not really buying
a house in year one.

54
00:02:44,901 --> 00:02:48,499
You are renting the money to buy it later.

55
00:02:48,499 --> 00:02:52,745
The crossover — the first
month where more of your

56
00:02:52,745 --> 00:02:56,206
payment goes to the house than to the bank

57
00:02:56,206 --> 00:02:58,431
— doesn't arrive until year thirteen.

58
00:02:58,431 --> 00:03:01,835
Half the term. And that's
if rates never move

59
00:03:01,835 --> 00:03:04,143
and you never remortgage.

60
00:03:04,143 --> 00:03:07,600
Every time you remortgage,
the clock resets

61
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,614
and you walk back into the
front-loaded years again.

62
00:03:11,224 --> 00:03:15,852
This is where the Qur'anic
ruling stops being abstract.

63
00:03:15,852 --> 00:03:18,147
The verse doesn't forbid profit.

64
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:21,287
It permits trade and forbids riba —

65
00:03:21,287 --> 00:03:24,006
in the same breath, in the same sentence.

66
00:03:24,006 --> 00:03:28,597
So the line isn't between
making money and not

67
00:03:28,597 --> 00:03:31,911
making money. It's between
two ways of making it.

68
00:03:31,911 --> 00:03:34,562
In trade, you own something,

69
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:37,389
you carry the risk of owning it,

70
00:03:37,389 --> 00:03:39,881
and you profit if it goes well.

71
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:43,352
In riba, you part with money, you

72
00:03:43,352 --> 00:03:47,812
carry no risk at all, and
your return is guaranteed

73
00:03:47,812 --> 00:03:49,936
in advance. Ownership and risk.

74
00:03:49,936 --> 00:03:53,748
Hold onto those two words,

75
00:03:53,748 --> 00:03:57,193
because every structure in the next half

76
00:03:57,193 --> 00:04:00,762
of this video is an attempt
to put them back into the

77
00:04:00,762 --> 00:04:03,357
transaction. Structure one:

78
00:04:03,357 --> 00:04:04,594
Murabaha. Cost-plus sale.

79
00:04:04,594 --> 00:04:07,545
The bank buys the property outright.

80
00:04:07,545 --> 00:04:10,115
It actually owns it —
title, risk, the lot.

81
00:04:10,115 --> 00:04:13,956
Then it sells that property
on to you at a price

82
00:04:13,956 --> 00:04:16,616
that's stated openly: the
cost, plus an agreed markup.

83
00:04:16,616 --> 00:04:19,857
You pay that total in
instalments over an agreed term.

84
00:04:19,857 --> 00:04:22,204
Two things follow from that.

85
00:04:22,204 --> 00:04:25,061
The bank took ownership, however briefly,

86
00:04:25,061 --> 00:04:28,711
and with it the risk. And your
price is fixed at signature.

87
00:04:28,711 --> 00:04:33,545
Two hundred and ninety
thousand pounds is two

88
00:04:33,545 --> 00:04:36,778
hundred and ninety thousand
pounds — it cannot grow.

89
00:04:36,778 --> 00:04:40,551
There is no compounding, because there's
no outstanding loan to compound.

90
00:04:40,551 --> 00:04:44,002
There's a sale price and a balance

91
00:04:44,002 --> 00:04:46,105
remaining on it. The
trade-off is rigidity.

92
00:04:46,105 --> 00:04:49,661
Fixed price cuts both ways:
settle early and you may not save

93
00:04:49,661 --> 00:04:53,422
much, because you owe a price,
not an interest-bearing balance.

94
00:04:54,393 --> 00:04:58,497
Structure two: Ijara wa Iqtina —
a lease that ends in ownership.

95
00:04:58,497 --> 00:05:01,956
Again the bank buys the
property and holds the title.

96
00:05:01,956 --> 00:05:05,504
But this time it doesn't sell it to

97
00:05:05,504 --> 00:05:07,971
you up front. It leases it to you.

98
00:05:07,971 --> 00:05:11,587
You pay rent for the use of an asset

99
00:05:11,587 --> 00:05:15,043
that someone else owns — which is

100
00:05:15,043 --> 00:05:18,387
an ordinary, permitted transaction.

101
00:05:18,387 --> 00:05:22,151
At the end of the term,
or progressively along

102
00:05:22,151 --> 00:05:25,013
the way, ownership transfers to you.

103
00:05:25,013 --> 00:05:28,513
Because the bank holds the title, it holds

104
00:05:28,513 --> 00:05:30,766
the obligations that come with it.

105
00:05:30,766 --> 00:05:34,085
Structural insurance, major repairs,

106
00:05:34,085 --> 00:05:37,279
and the loss if the
building is destroyed —

107
00:05:37,279 --> 00:05:40,875
those sit with the owner, not the

108
00:05:40,875 --> 00:05:43,742
tenant. That's not a technicality.

109
00:05:43,742 --> 00:05:47,306
That's the risk the whole ruling

110
00:05:47,306 --> 00:05:50,891
turns on, sitting where it should.

111
00:05:50,891 --> 00:05:53,627
The trade-off is that
rent can be reviewed.

112
00:05:53,627 --> 00:05:57,199
A poorly written Ijara lets the

113
00:05:57,199 --> 00:06:00,695
bank reprice the rent against a market

114
00:06:00,695 --> 00:06:05,044
benchmark — and if that
benchmark is just the base rate

115
00:06:05,044 --> 00:06:09,403
under a different name, you
have re-created the thing

116
00:06:09,403 --> 00:06:10,610
you were avoiding.

117
00:06:10,610 --> 00:06:14,489
Which brings us to the
structure most British

118
00:06:14,489 --> 00:06:17,224
and American Islamic banks actually use.

119
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:20,903
Structure three: Diminishing Musharaka.

120
00:06:20,903 --> 00:06:23,322
Shared, shrinking partnership.

121
00:06:23,322 --> 00:06:27,444
This is what Al Rayan, Gatehouse
and Guidance Residential

122
00:06:27,444 --> 00:06:31,211
are built on. You and the bank
buy the property together.

123
00:06:31,211 --> 00:06:34,482
You put in ten percent,
the bank puts in ninety.

124
00:06:34,482 --> 00:06:36,990
You are co-owners, on the title,

125
00:06:36,990 --> 00:06:39,149
in those proportions.

126
00:06:39,149 --> 00:06:43,204
Each month you make two
payments that are deliberately

127
00:06:43,204 --> 00:06:45,983
kept separate. The first is rent —

128
00:06:45,983 --> 00:06:48,717
but only on the share you don't own yet.

129
00:06:48,717 --> 00:06:52,270
The second buys a slice
of the bank's share

130
00:06:52,270 --> 00:06:55,234
and moves it to your side permanently.

131
00:06:55,234 --> 00:06:58,284
And here's the part worth sitting with.

132
00:06:58,284 --> 00:07:00,920
As your share grows, the
bank's share shrinks —

133
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:04,637
so the rent you owe on it
falls, every single month,

134
00:07:04,637 --> 00:07:05,997
automatically.

135
00:07:05,997 --> 00:07:10,270
Your position improves as
time passes rather than

136
00:07:10,270 --> 00:07:14,235
in spite of it. Compare
that to the amortisation

137
00:07:14,235 --> 00:07:17,646
chart from earlier, where
you were still underwater

138
00:07:17,646 --> 00:07:18,899
at year ten.

139
00:07:18,899 --> 00:07:21,537
Ownership is real, it's on the register,

140
00:07:21,537 --> 00:07:23,896
and the risk is shared
in proportion to it.

141
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:26,464
All three side by side.

142
00:07:26,464 --> 00:07:29,164
Murabaha gives you certainty —

143
00:07:29,164 --> 00:07:31,704
a fixed price you can plan around.

144
00:07:31,704 --> 00:07:35,227
Ijara gives you flexibility and puts the

145
00:07:35,227 --> 00:07:37,621
ownership burden squarely on the bank.

146
00:07:37,621 --> 00:07:41,048
Musharaka gives you a
cost that falls as you

147
00:07:41,048 --> 00:07:44,874
go, and it's the one most widely available

148
00:07:44,874 --> 00:07:48,513
in the West. None of them is automatically

149
00:07:48,513 --> 00:07:51,843
the best. They answer different questions.

150
00:07:52,769 --> 00:07:56,730
Now the part that matters most,
because not every product marketed

151
00:07:56,730 --> 00:08:00,872
as Islamic survives
contact with these four

152
00:08:00,872 --> 00:08:04,804
questions. One. Does the bank
actually take legal title,

153
00:08:04,804 --> 00:08:08,357
even for a moment? If it never
appears on the register,

154
00:08:08,357 --> 00:08:11,893
you have a loan with a different name

155
00:08:11,893 --> 00:08:13,117
on the paperwork. Two.

156
00:08:13,117 --> 00:08:15,854
Is the profit disclosed
and agreed at signing,

157
00:08:15,854 --> 00:08:18,757
or does it float against a benchmark?

158
00:08:18,757 --> 00:08:21,663
Floating isn't automatically fatal —

159
00:08:21,663 --> 00:08:25,074
but if it tracks the base
rate exactly, ask what

160
00:08:25,074 --> 00:08:27,641
the structure is really doing.

161
00:08:27,641 --> 00:08:31,205
Three. If the property is
destroyed and uninsured,

162
00:08:31,205 --> 00:08:34,806
who takes the loss? If the answer
is "you, entirely," then the bank

163
00:08:34,806 --> 00:08:38,950
never took ownership risk, whatever

164
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:41,323
the contract is called.

165
00:08:41,323 --> 00:08:43,869
Four — the one almost nobody asks.

166
00:08:43,869 --> 00:08:46,371
What happens if you pay late?

167
00:08:46,371 --> 00:08:49,827
In a genuine Islamic contract
a late charge cannot become

168
00:08:49,827 --> 00:08:53,356
profit for the bank, because
profit on delay is exactly riba.

169
00:08:53,356 --> 00:08:56,926
It must go to charity,
and the bank must be able

170
00:08:56,926 --> 00:08:58,294
to show you that it does.

171
00:08:58,294 --> 00:09:01,757
Ask them. The answer tells you more

172
00:09:01,757 --> 00:09:03,414
than the brochure ever will.

173
00:09:03,414 --> 00:09:06,468
And I'll be honest with you:

174
00:09:06,468 --> 00:09:10,173
there are serious scholars
who argue some of these

175
00:09:10,173 --> 00:09:14,113
products are conventional
lending with extra steps.

176
00:09:14,113 --> 00:09:16,921
That criticism isn't unreasonable.

177
00:09:16,921 --> 00:09:20,325
These four questions are
how you check a specific

178
00:09:20,325 --> 00:09:23,123
offer instead of trusting a label.

179
00:09:23,583 --> 00:09:26,816
So: a conventional mortgage
is a loan secured on a house,

180
00:09:26,816 --> 00:09:30,695
priced so that ten years in you've
paid a hundred and seventy-five

181
00:09:30,695 --> 00:09:33,568
thousand and still owe a
hundred and eighty-four.

182
00:09:33,568 --> 00:09:36,274
Murabaha replaces it with a sale.

183
00:09:36,274 --> 00:09:38,890
Ijara replaces it with a lease.

184
00:09:38,890 --> 00:09:42,315
Musharaka replaces it with a partnership

185
00:09:42,315 --> 00:09:45,806
that shrinks. All three
put ownership and risk

186
00:09:45,806 --> 00:09:47,961
back where the ruling requires them.

187
00:09:47,961 --> 00:09:51,474
You don't have to accept the hundred

188
00:09:51,474 --> 00:09:53,935
and eighty-eight thousand.

189
00:09:53,935 --> 00:09:58,205
You have to know which of the
three fits your situation

190
00:09:58,205 --> 00:10:00,145
— and then ask the four questions.

191
00:10:01,174 --> 00:10:04,688
If this changed how you read
your own mortgage statement,

192
00:10:04,688 --> 00:10:08,061
subscribe — a new mechanism
decoded every week.

193
00:10:08,061 --> 00:10:11,553
Next episode: whether the savings

194
00:10:11,553 --> 00:10:15,142
account you were told was the
safe option is quietly doing

195
00:10:15,142 --> 00:10:16,414
the same thing to you.

